{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"We need to evaluate this sum for $n$-values from $1$ through $4$. \n\nWe don't know the value of $k$, so we will leave that variable in our expressions.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^4 \\dfrac{k}{n+1}\u0026= \\dfrac{k}{(1)+1} + \\dfrac{k}{(2)+1} + \\dfrac{k}{(3)+1} + \\dfrac{k}{(4)+1}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026= \\dfrac{k}{2} + \\dfrac{k}{3} + \\dfrac{k}{4} + \\dfrac{k}{5}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^4 \\dfrac{k}{n+1}=\\,?$\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"radio 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"choices":[{"content":"$\\dfrac k2+\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac k4+\\dfrac k5$","correct":true},{"clue":"We should only substitute into $n$, because it is the index, and not into $k$.","content":"$\\dfrac 12+\\dfrac 23+\\dfrac 34+\\dfrac 45$","correct":false},{"clue":"Be sure to substitute into the complete expression before increasing the index. Also, we should only substitute into $n$, because it is the index, and not into $k$.","content":"$\\dfrac 1k+\\dfrac 2k+\\dfrac 3k+\\dfrac 4k$","isNoneOfTheAbove":false},{"clue":"This mixes up the $n$ and $k$ values.","content":"$\\dfrac{1}{k+1}+\\dfrac{2}{k+2}+\\dfrac{3}{k+3}+\\dfrac{4}{k+4}$","isNoneOfTheAbove":false}],"deselectEnabled":false,"displayCount":null,"hasNoneOfTheAbove":false,"multipleSelect":false,"randomize":false},"static":false,"type":"radio","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
